WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:11.970
Hi, I'm Devon.

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:12.990
Today, I'm going to walk you through

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:15.824
how I restore a 200-year-old
grandfather clock.

00:00:17.294 --> 00:00:19.894
[clock chiming]

00:00:19.894 --> 00:00:22.530
Clocks that old weren't branded

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:25.530
with anything other than
just the clockmaker's name.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:28.020
A single person built the entire thing.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:31.980
This particular one is Abraham
Shaw of Billingborough.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:33.690
So an English clock.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:36.060
My customer brought it
from England to the US.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.400
She inherited this from her parents,

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:39.690
who inherited it from their parents,

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:41.565
who inherited it from
their parents, back to,

00:00:41.565 --> 00:00:43.740
ballpark, 1820s.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:47.040
Pre-restoration, this clock
is probably not worth much.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.073
I'd say maybe $500 at most. Probably less.

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:52.977
The case definitely is in the need of

00:00:52.977 --> 00:00:54.720
more attention than the mechanism.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:56.220
The whole case has come apart.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:58.740
I like to start with a cleaning,

00:00:58.740 --> 00:00:59.888
just to make sure there's not going to be

00:00:59.888 --> 00:01:01.260
any kind of dust or debris,

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.630
or, with all clocks, spiderwebs,

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:07.489
that's going to be in the
way of re-gluing everything.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:09.990
The next step after I clean it

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:13.413
is start approaching
some of the wood repairs.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.670
I almost never have to fix a
case this bad of condition.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:21.360
This case was in serious need of help.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.840
The parts of a clock case,
the names of them vary.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.760
Each country would call
them different things.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:28.620
But typically for an English clock,

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:31.590
you would have the
bonnet, which is the hood.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:32.790
Basically just slides off.

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:34.140
It has the front door to it

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.810
and then typically some type of decoration

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:38.550
to make the clock look nice.

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:39.570
Then you have the trunk,

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:41.610
which is just the main body of the clock,

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:42.660
where if you have a door

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:44.520
you could see the
pendulum and the weights.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.040
The base of the clock is the bottom,

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.290
the bottom square section.

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:51.232
The floor of the clock was broken,

00:01:51.232 --> 00:01:52.598
and half of it missing.

00:01:52.598 --> 00:01:57.210
So I just do a couple quick
measurements on the inside

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:59.850
and cut out a new piece.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:04.290
This is the most common broken
thing on grandfather clocks.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.060
If there's some type of
failure from the weights,

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:07.920
the weights come crashing down,

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.690
and when you get a 200-year-old clock,

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:12.180
chances are it's missing the bottom.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:15.046
So I attach the floor to the base

00:02:15.046 --> 00:02:18.221
by using glue and nails.

00:02:18.221 --> 00:02:21.777
And then the nails just
help the glue dry properly.

00:02:23.435 --> 00:02:25.800
The top right trim piece of the hood

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.380
is often in very bad condition.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.090
I was left with decisions to make there,

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:32.433
and I just decided to
keep the original piece.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:35.220
It was a little difficult to tell

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.770
what was actually the
cause of that top corner,

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:40.200
but my best guess, and
a few other clockmakers

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.450
that gave me an idea, was
most likely bugs of some sort,

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:45.540
and then if it rotted from there.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:49.743
The first thing I needed
to do was copy its profile.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:52.680
There was no good place
to copy it on this piece

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.900
because it was damaged
pretty much all over.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:58.530
So I just went to the back
edge of the other side

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:01.390
and just used a piece
of paper and a pencil,

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:04.470
and I'd just get the
profile for that piece.

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:07.147
And then I glue that to
another piece of wood

00:03:07.147 --> 00:03:08.730
to cut that shape out.

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:09.630
And that piece of wood

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:12.903
is what we will end up
calling a form tool.

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:16.600
And then I use

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:19.558
a combination of Bondo
wood filler and paint

00:03:19.558 --> 00:03:22.590
just to get it close to
the right color initially.

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:25.530
And, of course, this clock
would've been stained originally,

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:27.120
so to try to match that look

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:29.730
I just used a combination of
brown, red, and black paint

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:33.540
to mimic the wood grain as best I could.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:35.130
I mix that up with the hardener

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:39.450
and apply it on in large,
copious amounts onto the piece

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:41.010
and make sure it's all rubbed in

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:42.840
to all the nooks and crannies,

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.570
so it's going to make a
good joint everywhere.

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:47.520
And then I use that form tool that I made

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:52.170
to basically wipe off the excess
other than where I want it.

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:54.000
I wouldn't call it fabrication,

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.550
but I did have to, for
lack of better terms,

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:58.113
fabricate that piece.

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:02.880
And then I cut it off to size.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.130
So I give it a 45-degree
angle on the front

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:06.360
where it will meet the other piece

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:08.010
and then cut the back off just straight

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:10.560
where it will be at the back of the clock.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.600
And then I glue it down.

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:14.790
In this case, I did not use wood glue

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:16.620
because I used Bondo wood filler

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:17.910
and I didn't want the wood filler

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:20.250
to make it so the joint
would not be strong.

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:23.760
So I used a general-purpose
glue, everything type of glue,

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:25.530
which I wouldn't use on
something load-bearing,

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:27.930
but this is just an ornate
decorative piece on the side.

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:30.363
So that glue will be sufficient for that.

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:34.020
Nine times out of 10,

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:36.723
wood glue is going to be your best option.

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.990
There's a lot of odd shapes on this,

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:41.880
so it's not easy to clamp.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.160
Clamps have to be
opposite from each other,

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:46.350
and you don't always
have that availability.

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:50.583
So I usually use weights or
something like that in place.

00:04:53.255 --> 00:04:54.960
Easily the hardest part of this case,

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.870
easily the hardest part, was the glass.

00:04:57.870 --> 00:05:01.380
That could have been glazed
in place 200 years ago,

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:03.720
and then at some point during its life,

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:04.830
then that glass broke,

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:06.900
so I'm left with a bunch of glazing

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:10.387
that's full of glass
shards all throughout it.

00:05:10.387 --> 00:05:14.250
So there's no great way to
get that old glazing out.

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:16.260
The method I use is
just take a razor blade

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.180
and scrape it out.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:20.940
You know, take your time going
around the whole perimeter.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:23.370
Clean up as you go to make
sure none of the glass shards

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:25.650
are going to hurt the piece of wood.

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:27.578
Removing that previous glazing

00:05:27.578 --> 00:05:32.154
is very tedious and can hurt you.

00:05:32.154 --> 00:05:34.060
And then I put the glass in.

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:38.160
And then I use a couple nails

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:41.670
to hold the glass in while I glaze it.

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:43.710
And it's straightforward

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:45.610
but definitely requires a steady hand.

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:52.770
I, in between, do work on the mechanism,

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:54.693
so, you know, disassemble it.

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:57.870
Disassembly takes a lot of focus.

00:05:57.870 --> 00:05:59.040
You have to make sure
you're paying attention

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:00.150
to where things are

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:03.150
and if you see any kind
of previous repairs,

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:06.480
because previous repairs are
usually a sign of disaster.

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:09.300
A lot of beginner clockmakers or hobbyists

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:11.670
are tempted to not
disassemble the movement

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:13.440
and dunk it straight into
the ultrasonic cleaner

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:15.180
thinking that it'll clean it.

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:16.590
You know, it's in there.

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:18.534
But you do need to take everything apart.

00:06:18.534 --> 00:06:19.950
Not as much for cleaning

00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:23.280
but more so for rinsing
and drying afterwards.

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:26.250
The solution will go in
between the little tiny parts

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:27.450
if they're still together,

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:30.693
and it won't come back out
when you try to rinse it.

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:34.260
You gotta take everything apart
down to the very last screw,

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:36.210
every last pin, and clean it that way

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:37.680
in the ultrasonic solution,

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:39.930
which needs to be clock-cleaning solution.

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:41.580
If it's just brass-cleaning solution

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:43.200
or something along those lines,

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:44.940
you can flash-rust the steel.

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:46.770
Or if you use steel-cleaning solution,

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:48.900
you can actually corrode the brass.

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:51.863
It sits there for, ballpark, 20 minutes.

00:06:51.863 --> 00:06:54.150
That solution stays on
the parts for too long,

00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:55.650
it will ruin them.

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:57.210
So you have to very, very thoroughly

00:06:57.210 --> 00:06:58.800
rinse it all off with water.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:00.870
And then water is just
as bad as the solution

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:02.010
if you don't dry it off.

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:04.470
If you get a little bit of
water stay stuck in a corner,

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:06.103
it'll destroy the clock

00:07:06.103 --> 00:07:07.703
within a matter of a couple of years.

00:07:08.736 --> 00:07:11.313
Now I'm going to repair the mechanism.

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:14.610
I start working on the pendulum.

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:17.550
The pendulum was already in
pieces when I received it.

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:20.160
The bob of the pendulum, the
brass circle at the bottom,

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:21.369
is actually how we determine

00:07:21.369 --> 00:07:23.160
how fast or slow a clock will run.

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:26.790
So it needs to be able to
slide up and down smoothly,

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:29.250
without any kind of
snagging or getting stuck.

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:32.612
So I had to remove a very
small amount of material

00:07:32.612 --> 00:07:35.040
from the rod to make sure
the bob could go up and down

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:37.260
and then polish that surface to make sure

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:39.453
it's as close to frictionless as possible.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:43.200
I actually found some witness marks

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:45.720
on this particular clock.

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:48.600
On the rod, there were
some lines scratched,

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:51.155
and I just went ahead and assumed

00:07:51.155 --> 00:07:53.760
that that's where they wanted
the pendulum bob set to.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:56.010
So I went ahead, set it right there,

00:07:56.010 --> 00:07:57.660
and put the screw on underneath it,

00:07:57.660 --> 00:07:59.790
and it actually worked first try.

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:02.883
So that witness mark was correct.

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:07.530
Next, I am polishing the pivots.

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:09.360
I'm not focusing on the gear.

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:11.940
I'm actually focusing on
the arbors for the gears.

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:13.290
At the end of each arbor,

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:16.462
there is a pivot point that it
will turn inside the plates.

00:08:16.462 --> 00:08:18.570
Its axle, basically.

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:20.070
Clock parts have a lot of terminology

00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:21.510
that don't match up with today.

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:24.777
Gears are not called gears,
they're called wheels.

00:08:24.777 --> 00:08:27.593
Axles are not called axles,
they're called arbors.

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:31.023
I use a lathe, which is convenient

00:08:31.023 --> 00:08:33.570
because it turns
everything concentrically.

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:34.770
Makes everything a lot easier

00:08:34.770 --> 00:08:36.750
and turns out nicer in the end.

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:40.200
A lathe is actively trying
to kill you at all times.

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:42.660
If you touch it, it will not stop.

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:46.500
So I keep my fingers out
of the way by using paper.

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:47.880
And then if I have enough room,

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:51.270
I actually use very fine abrasive stones

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:52.650
like an Arkansas stone,

00:08:52.650 --> 00:08:54.720
which is just very fine, fine grit.

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:56.946
We're talking around 4,000 grit.

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:00.480
If you leave jagged edges,

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:01.830
it doesn't happen overnight,

00:09:01.830 --> 00:09:03.630
but over the course of a few years

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:05.790
the clock will actually eat itself away

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:08.906
if it's not smooth, smooth surfaces.

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:14.850
The other end of them is their bearings

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:15.960
inside the plates.

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:17.815
There's little holes in the plates.

00:09:17.815 --> 00:09:19.200
Those need the same process.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:20.640
They need to be cleaned very thoroughly

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:23.281
and then made sure that
they're polished properly

00:09:23.281 --> 00:09:25.883
where it'll have very little friction.

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:29.023
The escapement pallet is going to be

00:09:29.023 --> 00:09:31.320
one of the most-ignored things in a clock,

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:33.840
but arguably the most important.

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:36.813
This is what the pendulum is
attached to at the very top.

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:38.700
That groove,

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:42.450
that's a fraction of the
thickness of a human hair.

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:44.073
It's really, really small.

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:47.490
The escape wheel has been hitting that

00:09:47.490 --> 00:09:50.460
over and over again for many, many years.

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:52.530
The clock will not run accurately

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:54.600
if those grooves are there.

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:58.440
Most people, when they repair
clocks, will ignore this.

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:01.413
But I go ahead and
re-polish those surfaces.

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:04.740
We're putting in brass cable.

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:06.600
When I picked it up, it
already had brass wire,

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:08.970
but originally it would've
had cat-gut cable,

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:10.890
which would've been made out of cat guts.

00:10:10.890 --> 00:10:13.380
That's exactly what it sounds like.

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:14.760
We use brass instead of steel,

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:17.370
because steel cable will actually tear up

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:19.080
the parts inside the clock.

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:21.630
But brass is what the
parts are made out of,

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:24.180
so it's hard to tear up brass with brass.

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:26.528
You want this to wrap around

00:10:26.528 --> 00:10:27.930
the barrel that it's going onto

00:10:27.930 --> 00:10:29.670
the correct amount of times.

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:31.890
And you also want it to have the weights

00:10:31.890 --> 00:10:34.080
reach the bottom of
the case in eight days.

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:35.580
This is an eight-day movement.

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:39.120
There's a few things
you've got to be careful of

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:40.340
when assembling the mechanism.

00:10:40.340 --> 00:10:41.400
So, the obvious one

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:43.350
of make sure everything's
in the right place,

00:10:43.350 --> 00:10:45.960
but that's actually a lot less
like a puzzle than you think.

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:47.880
If you know what each part does,

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:50.010
it only has one place to go.

00:10:50.010 --> 00:10:52.350
And then the difficult
part is indexing it,

00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:54.300
where you don't just put
it in the right spot,

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:57.270
but it needs to be oriented
correctly when it goes there.

00:10:57.270 --> 00:10:59.760
So you need to have one part of the gear

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:02.250
meshing with another part of the gear.

00:11:02.250 --> 00:11:06.450
All those pivots that we
polished are very fragile.

00:11:06.450 --> 00:11:08.790
So when you are putting
the plates together,

00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:10.080
you have to be very careful

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:12.090
and use very small amounts of force

00:11:12.090 --> 00:11:14.430
to try to nudge everything into its place

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:17.015
and make sure that the pivots
go into the correct holes

00:11:17.015 --> 00:11:19.268
and aren't damaged.

00:11:19.268 --> 00:11:22.980
That's definitely the
art of fixing the clock,

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:25.230
is trying to close the plates.

00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:27.510
It takes a certain feel.

00:11:27.510 --> 00:11:30.150
Once it's together, then you
get it up on the test stand

00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:32.318
and make sure it's still working right.

00:11:32.318 --> 00:11:34.230
[clock chiming]

00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:38.550
And then there's actually a
several-week period of testing.

00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:39.660
Every time I come into the shop,

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:43.200
I see if it's running fast or
slow and make an adjustment.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:44.910
Usually when I test it there,

00:11:44.910 --> 00:11:47.700
I like to get it to where
everything is working

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:51.504
completely properly for
a whole week straight.

00:11:51.504 --> 00:11:53.790
Then I put it into the
case to test it there,

00:11:53.790 --> 00:11:56.475
to make sure it's all going
to continue to work properly.

00:11:57.420 --> 00:11:59.550
Time at the workbench,

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:03.570
I spent probably around two weeks or so

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:05.070
actually working on stuff.

00:12:05.070 --> 00:12:08.433
You know, and that's, I'm
talking full hours of two weeks.

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:11.400
I'm pretty happy with this restoration.

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:13.560
Definitely the movement was, you know,

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:16.710
everything went according
to plan, how I liked.

00:12:16.710 --> 00:12:18.930
The most satisfying part
of this restoration,

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:21.390
and every restoration,

00:12:21.390 --> 00:12:24.255
is definitely going to
be one of two things.

00:12:24.255 --> 00:12:27.420
Either the first time the clock ticks,

00:12:27.420 --> 00:12:30.240
you know, when I set it up and it works.

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:32.820
That's extremely satisfying
to have that happen.

00:12:32.820 --> 00:12:35.250
And the other part is the client

00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:36.480
when I deliver it back.

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:38.160
It's extremely satisfying

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:40.590
to see these people's reactions.

00:12:40.590 --> 00:12:43.365
Sometimes, for clocks
that are chiming clocks,

00:12:43.365 --> 00:12:45.240
I'll bring it back and
they haven't heard it chime

00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:48.573
since they were 8 years old
in their grandparents' house.

00:12:48.573 --> 00:12:51.300
[clock chiming]

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:54.637
You're only the temporary
caretaker of this clock.

00:12:54.637 --> 00:12:56.940
It's been around for 200 years.

00:12:56.940 --> 00:12:58.350
It's going to outlive you.

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:03.350
So it's more important to do
what is right for the clock.

00:13:03.810 --> 00:13:05.130
I try to keep it original

00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:07.130
as much as I possibly can at all times.

